MATH_0004>.\nMoreover we assume given a morphism of ringed spaces\n<MATH_0005>, an <MATH_0006>-module\n<MATH_0007>, and an <MATH_0008>-map <MATH_0009>\nof sheaves of modules (Sheaves, Definition <REF_0001>\nand Section <REF_0002>).\nIn this section we ask ourselves whether we can find\nthe question mark fitting into the following diagram\n<MATH_0010>\n(where the vertical arrows are <MATH_0011>-maps)\nand moreover how unique the solution is (if it exists). More precisely,\nwe look for a first order thickening\n<MATH_0012>\nand a morphism of thickenings <MATH_0013> as in\n(<REF_0003>)\nwhere <MATH_0014> is identified with <MATH_0015>\nsuch that <MATH_0016> induces the given map <MATH_0017>.\nWe will say <MATH_0018> is a <TEXTITOPEN_0001>solution<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> to\n(<REF_0004>).\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:3694dfbc4e3e24a5d7c18849ab3db963667f78ce1e9d88cc8f0d953ec3592107", "unit_id": "tag:08U6:prose-0001"}
